<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Merged into the generated Info.plist by the Tauri bundler, which picks this
  file up automatically because it sits next to tauri.conf.json (the alternative
  is bundle.macOS.infoPlist, which only takes a path to exactly this).

  These are the TCC (Privacy) usage strings. macOS shows one when the app first
  touches a protected folder — and a project directory is almost always inside
  one, since ~/Documents is where people keep code. WITHOUT the matching key the
  prompt appears with no reason attached, which reads as an app grabbing at your
  files for nothing, so "Don't Allow" is the natural click.

  Getting that answer is silent and total: the deny is recorded per bundle id in
  the TCC database, macOS never asks twice, and every `claude` Mulpex spawns then
  gets a cwd it cannot even getcwd() ("Operation not permitted"). Claude exits 1
  within the same second, the poll loop reaps the session, and the row vanishes
  from the sidebar about 100 ms after appearing. Nothing is logged and no error
  surfaces anywhere in the app — it just looks like Claude refuses to start.
  (Measured 2026-08-05: every project lived under ~/Documents, all 8 spawns on a
  launch exited rc=1, and the same build opened a project in /private/tmp fine.)

  A rebuild changes the ad-hoc signature, so macOS may re-ask on a new version —
  which is exactly why the prompt needs to justify itself. These keys only supply
  that text; they request nothing and grant nothing on their own.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSDocumentsFolderUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Mulpex needs access to open project folders you keep in Documents and to run Claude Code sessions inside them.</string>
	<key>NSDesktopFolderUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Mulpex needs access to open project folders you keep on the Desktop and to run Claude Code sessions inside them.</string>
	<key>NSDownloadsFolderUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Mulpex needs access to open project folders you keep in Downloads and to run Claude Code sessions inside them.</string>
	<key>NSNetworkVolumesUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Mulpex needs access to open project folders on network volumes and to run Claude Code sessions inside them.</string>
	<key>NSRemovableVolumesUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Mulpex needs access to open project folders on external drives and to run Claude Code sessions inside them.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
